Possibly from {{inh|ja|jpx-pro||*jənaC-|sort=よね}} (Vovin, 1998)<ref>Whitman, John (2012). "[https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12284-011-9080-0 Northeast Asian Linguistic Ecology and the Advent of Rice Agriculture in Korea and Japan], ''Rice'', Volume 4, Issue 3–4, pp 149–158. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12284-011-9080-0</ref> and related to {{m|ja|稲||tr=ine|[[rice]] [[plant]]}}.

Unknown "-C-" consonantal segment, reconstructed by Vovin, seems unlikely considering {{IPAfont|[a ~ e]}} vowel alternation, seen in bound form ''yona-'' and free form ''yone'':<ref>Salingre,  Maëlys ''[https://www.researchgate.net/publication/335336036_Apophonic_toponyms_in_Japanese Apophonic toponyms in Japanese]'' 2019</ref>
:Compare {{m|ja|藍||tr=awi → ai|indigo}} from {{m|ja|青||tr=awo → ao|[[blue]]}} + {{m|ja|い|tr=i|pos=emphatic nominative particle{{rfv-etym|ja}}}}<ref>Thomas Pellard (2013).  [https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-01289288/document Ryukyuan perspectives on the proto-Japonic vowel system].  Frellesvig, Bjarke; Sells, Peter. Japanese/Korean Linguistics 20, CSLI Publications, pp.81–96, 2013.</ref> against {{m|ja|白い}} {{IPAfont|/siroi/}} from {{IPAfont|⟨siro<sub>1</sub>ki<sub>1</sub>⟩}}.<ref>Hamano, S. "Voicing of Obstruents in Old Japanese: Evidence from the Sound-Symbolic Stratum." ''Journal of East Asian Linguistics'' (2000) 9. 3: 207-225. https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1008367619295</ref>

The colloquial sense is derived from the components of the {{ja-l||米}} kanji: {{m|ja|八||tr=hachi|[[eight]]}} + {{m|ja|十||tr=jū|[[ten]]}} + {{m|ja|八||tr=hachi|eight}}.

